# Shell scripts MUST stay LF. bash aborts on CRLF with
#   "syntax error near unexpected token `$'{\r'"
# which makes scripts/update.sh unrunnable in production — and update.sh is the
# only deploy path (the admin API spawns it detached). A checkout on Windows
# with core.autocrlf=true writes CRLF into the working tree, so the alignment
# has to be stated here rather than left to each machine's git config.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Python is run on Linux (prod) but edited on Windows. A CRLF working tree
# makes `git diff` on prod show the whole file as rewritten, which hides the
# real change inside 1600+ fake lines — and upload paths that bypass git
# (scp/upload) write CRLF straight into the prod working tree. Normalise.
*.py text eol=lf
